   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the elder law, in relation to the state office for the aging\nsexual discrimination training program",
    "basePrintNo": "S5958",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S5958-2019",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [
      {
       "alternate": false,
       "chamber": "SENATE",
       "districtCode": 7,
       "fullName": "Anna Kaplan",
       "imgName": "1044_Anna_Kaplan.jpg",
       "incumbent": false,
       "memberId": 1224,
       "sessionMemberId": 1222,
       "sessionYear": 2019,
       "shortName": "KAPLAN"
      }
     ],
     "size": 1
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §203, Eld L",
    "lawSection": "Elder Law",
    "memo": "\n \nBILL NUMBER: S5958\n\nSPONSOR: MAY\n \nTITLE OF BILL:  An act to amend the elder law, in relation to the\nstate office for the aging sexual discrimination training program\n\n \nPURPOSE OR GENERAL IDEA OF BILL:\n\nTo require employees working with older adults to take an LGBT competen-\ncy training and require SOFA to regularly update their intake form to be\nLGBT-friendly.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF SPECIFIC PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1: Amends section 203 of the Elder Law by adding two new subdi-\nvisions 13 and 14.\n\nSubdivision 13 requires the director of the State Office for the Aging\n(SOFA) to establish or approve an LGBT competency training program.\nOutlines the requirements for an acceptable training program and lists\nwho must complete the training program.\n\nSubdivision 14 requires the director to consult with LGBT advocacy\ngroups to update the intake form Area Agencies on Aging use to reflect\ncurrent professional standards.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nThe LGBT older adult population continues to grow and has a unique set\nof realities and needs. LGBT older adults are more likely to be single,\nlive alone, and not have children. These factors can contribute to\nfinancial insecurity, a reliance on formal caregiving and services, and\nsocial isolation, which puts them at greater risk of developing health\nand mental health issues. A history of victimization and discrimination\nbecause of sexual orientation or gender identity lead many LGBT older\nadults to hide their identity out of fear of receiving inferior care or\nbeing turned away from services.  Employees who work with LGBT older\nadults need specialized training on the unique needs of this population\nin order to provide the best care. The training this bill requires would\nraise the professionalism, competence, and compassion of staff who work\nwith the LGBT older adult population.\n\nThe intake form currently used by all Area Agencies on Aging is called\n\"COMPASS\" (Comprehensive Assessment for Aging Network Community-Based\nLong Term Care Services). According to SOFA, 7% of service participants\ndid not answer questions related to sexual orientation or gender identi-\nty. This bill requires SOFA to work with LGBT advocacy groups to update\nthe language used in questions, definitions, and categories relating to\nsexual orientation and gender identity to reflect current standards.\nUpdating the form to be more LGBT-friendly will encourage individuals to\nself-identify which will help the aging network provide relevant, quali-\nty services to LGBT older adults.;.;\n\n \nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nNew bill.\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nTo be determined.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\n180 days after becoming law.\n",
